# **`.claude/*`, NOT `.claude/` — the trailing form cannot be re-included from.**
# git does not descend into an excluded DIRECTORY, so a `!` rule underneath one never
# fires. Excluding the contents instead lets the negation below work. Checked with
# `git check-ignore -v`, which reported the first spelling still ignoring the file.
.claude/*
# **The shared project settings TRAVEL; everything else in .claude/ does not.**
#
# Upstream ignores `.claude/` wholesale, which made the permission allowlist and the
# UserPromptSubmit hook per-machine — and Doug switches machines twice a week. A hook
# that is simply absent on the other machine fails SILENTLY: he captures, asks "what is
# this?", and gets a confident answer about the wrong subject.
#
# `settings.local.json` stays ignored, which is the split that file exists for: anything
# genuinely machine-specific belongs there, not here.
!.claude/settings.json
.vscode/
dev/
gh-pages/
docs/*/book/
*.fmu
